:: MoreChores [childhood]
# Contributing to the Tribe

"What else can I help with?" you ask your mother, eager to be useful and learn more skills.

She smiles at your enthusiasm. "There is always work to be done in the tribe. Come, you can help me prepare hides for making new clothing."

Your mother shows you how to scrape a deer hide with a stone tool, removing bits of flesh and fat that remain after the initial butchering. "If we don't clean the hide properly, it will rot and smell," she explains. "But if we prepare it well, it will become soft and strong for making clothes, containers, or shelter coverings."

The work is harder than it looks, requiring both strength and careful attention. You must scrape firmly enough to clean the hide but not so hard that you damage it. Your mother guides your hands, showing you the right pressure and motion.

As you work together, other women join you, bringing their own hides to prepare. They talk and sometimes sing as they work, sharing news and stories. You realize that these work sessions are also important social times when knowledge is shared and tribal bonds are strengthened.

By the time you finish, your arms are tired but you feel proud of your contribution. Your mother inspects your work and nods approvingly. "You have good hands for this," she says. "With practice, you will become skilled."

